## Java Code

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        int n = sc.nextInt();
        int[] arr = new int[n];

        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            arr[i] = sc.nextInt();
        }

        /*
         Concept:
         Rotated Sorted Array

         Sorted rotated array has
         at most one break point:
         arr[i] > arr[i+1]
        */

        int breaks = 0;
        int pivot = 0;

        for(int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            if(arr[i] > arr[i + 1]) {
                breaks++;
                pivot = i + 1;
            }
        }

        if(breaks == 0) {
            System.out.println(0);
        }
        else if(breaks > 1) {
            System.out.println(-1);
        }
        else {
            System.out.println(Math.min(pivot, n - pivot));
        }
    }
}
```

### Concepts Involved

```text
✓ Rotated Sorted Array
✓ Array Traversal
✓ Pivot Finding
✓ Break Point Detection
✓ Greedy (minimum of left/right rotations)
```

The main trick is recognizing that a sortable-by-rotation array can have **at most one break point**.
